WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.470
Alice and Bob have figured
out an amazing trick.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.960
They're exchanging
messages by plucking

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.620
a wire either hard or soft to
transmit a zero versus a one.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.160
However, due to gusts of
wind, false zeros or ones

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.020
can occur during transmission,
resulting in errors.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.312
Though they figured out a
way to communicate error

00:00:29.312 --> 00:00:33.560
free, even in the
presence of noise.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.410
How could they do this?

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.530
In the 1940s, Richard Hamming
faced a similar problem

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.760
while working at
Bell Laboratories.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.650
At the Bell Telephone
Laboratories, we do about 10%

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.700
of the experiments on a
computer, and about 90%

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.500
in the laboratory.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.110
Highly expected in time we will
do 90% on the computer and 10%

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.290
in the lab.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.540
Speed, cost, and effort
favor the computer

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.920
over the laboratory approach.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.440
At the time, the computers
used stored information

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.110
on punch cards representing
one versus zero

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.580
with hole versus no hole.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.610
This system was error prone,
because it was common for cards

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.630
to get bent or mis punched
in the first place.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.190
So holes could be
missed or no holes

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.670
could be accidentally
punctured, causing flipped bits.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.460
These errors would
cause the entire system

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.580
to halt until the error location
could be found and corrected

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.750
manually.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.760
So Hamming took it upon himself
to devise a method which

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.340
could automatically detect
and correct single bit

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.820
errors without
interrupting calculations.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.440
His solution was rooted in the
intuitive idea of repetition,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.860
something we all do when
faced with interference,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:00.080
or the chance that part of
our message will be corrupted.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.360
His error correcting
codes were built

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.030
on the simple concept
of a parity bit.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.710
A parity it is a
single bit which

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.690
is added to the end of
a message and indicates

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.980
whether the number of ones in
the message is even or odd.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.270
If a single error
occurs, the receiver

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.350
can then detect it
because the parity

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.530
bit will no longer match.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.840
However, to detect and
correct single errors,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.330
Hamming needed to
add more parity bits

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.190
to identify the error location.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.760
This leads to his seven four
code, which adds three parity

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.650
bits to each block of
four data bits as follows.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.370
First, we start with
the three parity bits,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.960
which can be
represented by a circle.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.500
Now, these circles intersect
to produce four regions,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.920
and the four data
bits are placed

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.780
inside these regions
in a specific order.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.840
Now, to calculate
the parity bits,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.900
we look at each
circle one at a time,

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.620
each containing three data bits.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.180
We determine the
parity bit as before.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.110
Add up the data bits, and
if we get zero or two,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:26.200
the parity bit is zero for even,
and if we get one or three,

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.692
the parity is one for odd.

00:03:28.692 --> 00:03:32.280
And we do this for all circles,
and end up with three parity

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.100
bits to match the
four data bits.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.970
These are then placed in a
standard sequence as follows.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.110
Realize now this system
can automatically

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:52.540
correct single errors
with a simple rule.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.890
If a single character occurs,
two or more of the parity bits

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:01.280
will be incorrect, and
wherever they intersect

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.670
is the location of the error.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:08.040
This intersecting data bit
is then flipped automatically

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.690
so that all parity
bits are valid again.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.250
And this is Alice
and Bob's trick,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:18.180
the additional parity bits
are known as redundant bits,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.920
because they don't carry
any new information.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.600
And all air correction codes
work this way, they all

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.370
increase the size of
the source messages

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.260
slightly at the expense of
automatically correcting

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.770
errors.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.430
We also use error correcting
codes for storage, for example,

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:40.040
on a physical CD,
the information

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.740
is encoded using special codes
to correct for chunks of errors

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:49.750
caused by scratches or dust,
which corrupt longer sequences

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.620
of zeros and ones
stored on the surface.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.410
This is why you can
scratch a CD and often it

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.260
will still play perfectly.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.030
Claude Shannon used
this idea of redundancy

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.610
to redefine the capacity
of a communication channel,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.440
because as the noise on
your channel increases,

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.840
we must increase the
amount of redundancy

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.980
to communicate error
free, and this must then

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.730
decrease the effective
amount of information

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.710
you can send per unit time.

